 ],
 "literal": "Soft-natured young woman, your good heart suffering, / if I agree to abandon you, having abandoned me, / days when suppliants do not come / may they become many, indeed, the fitness of my undertaking the journey.",
 "translation": "O soft-natured young woman! If I can tolerate abandoning you, leaving your good heart to suffer in loneliness, then let the days when suppliants abandon me and do not come to seek charity become many. That indeed would be the fitting consequence of my undertaking this journey.",
 "modern": "மென்மையான இயல்புடைய பெண்ணே! உன்னுடைய நல்ல நெஞ்சம் வருந்துமாறு உன்னைத் பிரிந்து செல்ல நான் உடன்படுவேனானால், என்னை விடுத்து இரவலர்கள் வராத நாட்கள் பலவாகட்டும்; அதுவே நான் மேற்கொள்ளும் இப்பயணத்திற்குத் தகுந்த பயனாகும்.",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the hero, who is contemplating a journey through the arid wasteland to acquire wealth. He addresses his beloved, a soft-natured young woman, acknowledging that his departure would leave her good heart in deep loneliness and grief. He makes a solemn vow: if he is cold-hearted enough to leave her suffering just to seek wealth, let him be cursed with the worst fate for a generous householder—that suppliants abandon him and no one comes to seek charity. In classical Tamil culture, earning wealth is meant for sharing with others; thus, a journey that destroys love and fails to enable charity is completely worthless. Through this powerful oath, he expresses his deep love and implies that he cannot bear to leave her.",
